# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9259> ,
              <https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/> ,
              <https://www.raa.se/evenemang-och-upplevelser/upplev-kulturarvet/varldsarv-i-sverige/> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/V%C3%A4rldsarv_i_Danmark> ,
              <https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1207/interior-department-celebrates-selection-of-pennsylvania-s-historic-moravian-bethlehem-district-to-unesco-world-heritage-list.htm> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Selection from Wikidata (query 1): every item with a P1435 (heritage designation) statement whose value is World Heritage Site (Q9259) and whose P17 (country) is Sweden (Q34), Norway (Q20), Denmark (Q35), Finland (Q33), Iceland (Q189), Greenland (Q223) or the Faroe Islands (Q4628), with its P757 (World Heritage Site ID), the start time (P580) of the designation, and its English and Swedish labels. The raw CSV was saved. The query returned 66 rows: transnational sites once per Nordic country, components of sites that carry their own designation (four Hjemmeluft rock-art panels in Alta without an ID, Drottningholm Palace, its theatre, Kina slott and park with IDs 559-001…559-004, Stora Sjöfallet National Park 774-003, the Falu koppargruva item that shares ID 1027), and items that describe the same property twice (Christiansfeld, 1468, and Moravian Church Settlements, 1468bis). These were reduced to one item per World Heritage property (one property number), choosing the item that stands for the whole property: Royal Domain of Drottningholm (Q28246887), Falun och Kopparbergslagen (Q636194), Laponia (Q1137314) and Moravian Church Settlements (Q128009257). No site was found in the Faroe Islands. That gives 44 properties: 13 only in Sweden, 7 only in Norway, 10 in Denmark and 3 in Greenland, 6 only in Finland, 3 in Iceland, High Coast / Kvarken Archipelago (Sweden and Finland) and the Struve Geodetic Arc (Norway, Sweden, Finland and seven countries outside the Nordic countries)."@en ,
                 "Recency: the selection was checked against the 2025 (47th) and 2026 (48th) sessions of the World Heritage Committee. UNESCO's site did not answer from the research environment (every request returned a \"503 Service Unavailable\" page, query 2), so the Internet Archive's captures were used (queries 3–6): the World Heritage List XML export captured on 2025-07-16, the last day of the 47th session, with all 42 Nordic properties then on the List (the parse_whc.py script kept the <row> elements whose <iso_code>, split on commas, contains se, no, dk, fi, is, gl or fo, and printed id_number, date_inscribed, secondary_dates, site, states, iso_code, category, transboundary and danger), including Møns Klint, inscribed in 2025; the New Inscribed Properties page of the 48th session (2026), captured 2026-10-01, which lists two new Nordic properties, Aalto Works (Finland) and The Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord (Denmark), and boundary or criteria changes only for properties outside the Nordic countries; and the States Parties pages of Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland and Iceland captured between 2026-08-27 and 2026-09-29, after that session, whose lists of properties (read by parse_states.py from the page's links to /en/list/<number>, each number with its link text; 15, 8, 13, 8 and 3 entries, with the names UNESCO now uses) match the 44 cards exactly. UNESCO's names on those pages are the same as in the 2025 XML, so no Nordic property was renamed in 2025–2026 after Christiansfeld became part of Moravian Church Settlements (2024)."@en ,
                 "Labels and claims (query 7): fetch_entities.py fetched, from the Wikidata API, the English, Swedish and multilingual labels, the aliases, the Swedish and English Wikipedia sitelinks and the P757, P17 and P1435 claims (with qualifiers) of the 47 candidate items; queries 8 and 9 fetched the labels of the countries, of the Wadden Sea itself (Q26080), of the Moravian Church (Q159318) and of the places named in the translated Swedish names."@en ,
                 "English names (fronts): UNESCO's official English name of each property as it stands on the States Parties pages, or a faithful short form of it where the official name has a subtitle: Kujataa (\"Kujataa Greenland: Norse and Inuit Farming at the Edge of the Ice Cap\"), Aasivissuit–Nipisat (\"… Inuit Hunting Ground between Ice and Sea\"), Vatnajökull National Park (\"… – Dynamic Nature of Fire and Ice\") and Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord (\"The … – Evolution and Climate Adaptation in the Earliest Eocene\"); for those four the short form is Wikidata's English label, except the last, whose label is the full name. Where the name on the front is also a Wikidata English label or alias, the builder checked it. Twelve fronts are UNESCO's official names in a form that is not a Wikidata label or alias (Church Town of Gammelstad, Luleå; Laponian Area; Agricultural Landscape of Southern Öland; Mining Area of the Great Copper Mountain in Falun; Grimeton Radio Station, Varberg; High Coast / Kvarken Archipelago; Rock Art of Alta; Vegaøyan – The Vega Archipelago; Jelling Mounds, Runic Stones and Church; Fortress of Suomenlinna; Verla Groundwood and Board Mill; Bronze Age Burial Site of Sammallahdenmäki); they were kept because the deck names properties as UNESCO does, and a name is a fact (see licensing). For West Norwegian Fjords – Geirangerfjord and Nærøyfjord the front gives only the two fjords' names (Wikidata's labels of Q193989 and Q212205, parts P527 of the property), because the full name gives the answer away; the full name is in the note."@en ,
                 "Swedish names (fronts): for the 15 Swedish properties, the Wikidata Swedish label, checked against the names used by Riksantikvarieämbetet (the Swedish National Heritage Board) on its pages \"Världsarv i Sverige\" and \"Frågor och svar om Världsarv\" (query 10); 13 agree, and for two the board writes another form of the same name (\"Grimeton radiostation\" for Radiostationen i Grimeton, \"Hälsingegårdarna\" for Hälsingegårdar), see quality control. For the other countries, the Wikidata Swedish label (or multilingual label: Møns Klint, Aalto Works), checked against the Swedish Wikipedia's lists \"Världsarv i Danmark\", \"Världsarv i Norge\" and \"Världsarv i Finland\" and article introductions (queries 11–13; each quoted revision is linked). Where Wikidata has no Swedish name, the compiler translated UNESCO's English name into Swedish using the Swedish names of the places in it, each checked in Wikidata: Vadehavet (the Swedish label of the Wadden Sea itself, Q26080), Herrnhutarnas bosättningar (herrnhutarna, an alias of the Moravian Church, Q159318), Vikingatida ringborgar and Benfiskfossilen i västra Limfjorden (Q3801); the Swedish front of the West Norwegian Fjords is \"Geirangerfjorden och Nærøyfjorden\", the Swedish labels of the two fjords (Q193989, Q212205). For the par force hunting landscape the front follows the title of the Swedish Wikipedia's article, \"Parforcejaktlandskap på norra Själland\", rather than the Wikidata label \"Parforcejaktslandskap på Norra Själland\" (capital N, which Swedish does not use for \"norra\"). The Swedish Wikipedia's lists use equivalent Swedish names (\"Ringborgar från vikingatiden\", \"Christiansfeld, en herrnhutnisk bosättning\"). Swedish typography: between the two place names of Rjukan–Notodden and Aasivissuit–Nipisat the Swedish fronts use an en dash (tankstreck), as the English fronts and the Swedish Wikipedia's introduction of Industriminnet Rjukan–Notodden do, where the Wikidata Swedish labels have a hyphen; those two labels are therefore not checked. For Røros the Wikidata label \"Bergsstaden Røros\" is kept, while the Swedish Wikipedia's article is titled \"Bergstaden Røros\" (the Norwegian spelling); see quality control."@en ,
                 "Backs: the Nordic country or countries the property lies in, from Wikidata P17, checked against UNESCO's States Parties. For the three properties in Greenland, Wikidata's P17 is Greenland and UNESCO's State Party is Denmark; the back reads \"Denmark (Greenland)\" / \"Danmark (Grönland)\". Transnational properties name every Nordic country they lie in (High Coast / Kvarken Archipelago: Sweden and Finland; Struve Geodetic Arc: Norway, Sweden and Finland, from north to south; Wadden Sea and Moravian Church Settlements: Denmark), and their notes name the other countries (Wikidata P17 and UNESCO's states)."@en ,
                 "Notes: the year of inscription, from the start time of Wikidata's P1435 statement, checked against UNESCO's date_inscribed (XML) or the 48th session page; extensions from Wikidata's P793 \"UNESCO World Heritage Site record modification\" statements with point in time P585 (Røros 2010, High Coast/Kvarken 2006, Wadden Sea 2014; Wikidata also gives 2011 for the Wadden Sea, and does not say which part was added: that the 2014 extension was the Danish part comes from UNESCO's secondary_dates and the Swedish Wikipedia, verification sources, as a fact in the compiler's words), checked against UNESCO's secondary_dates; the 2024 extension of Moravian Church Settlements, which Wikidata does not record, from the U.S. National Park Service's news release of 26 July 2024 (public domain), checked against UNESCO's secondary_dates and the XML row. A few notes add one line of context in the compiler's own words: Skogskyrkogården (a cemetery in Stockholm, Wikidata P131), Aalto Works (13 works by Alvar, Aino and Elissa Aalto: Wikidata's 13 P527 parts and their P84 architects), Þingvellir (the Swedish alias Tingvalla nationalpark) and the West Norwegian Fjords (the full official name). The builder cannot check qualifier values, so the years were compared by hand for all 44 cards (round 0)."@en ,
                 "Machine checks: for every card the builder checked against live Wikidata that the item is designated a World Heritage Site (P1435 Q9259), its World Heritage Site ID (P757), each Nordic country on the back (P17) and the English and Swedish labels of those countries; the English and Swedish names on the front wherever they are Wikidata labels or aliases (the builder accepts a multilingual label for any language: Møns Klint and Aalto Works have only a mul Swedish label); and for transnational sites the other countries in the note. For translated Swedish names it checks the Swedish labels of the places named. Then the deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators."@en ,
                 "Excluded: tentative-list sites and nominations not inscribed (for example the Faroe Islands' nominations), components of serial properties as cards of their own, properties whose only Nordic link is historical, and the Laponian Area's and Drottningholm's component items. Nothing in the deck depends on the World Heritage Centre's text: only facts (membership of the List, property number, States Parties, years, official names) were compared with it."@en ,
                 "Selection: Every property on UNESCO's World Heritage List on 4 October 2026 that lies wholly or partly in Sweden, Norway, Denmark (including Greenland; the Faroe Islands have no inscribed property), Finland or Iceland: 44 properties, each counted once, including Møns Klint (2025), Aalto Works and The Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord (both 2026). Transnational properties are one card whose back names every Nordic country they lie in, with the non-Nordic countries in the note. Left out: properties on tentative lists or nominated but not inscribed; component parts of serial properties (Wikidata gives some their own designation, e.g. Drottningholm Palace Theatre or Stora Sjöfallet National Park) as cards of their own; and Christiansfeld as a card of its own, since it has been part of the transnational property Moravian Church Settlements since 2024. The back gives the country, not the region or municipality; for Greenland it gives Denmark, the State Party, with Greenland in brackets."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The content sources are Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0, and one U.S. National Park Service news release, a U.S. Government work in the public domain: the selection (every item designated a World Heritage Site in the Nordic countries), the countries, the years of inscription and of the extensions (the Moravian Church Settlements' 2024 extension from the National Park Service), the English and Swedish names that are Wikidata labels or aliases, the facts in the notes, and the Swedish place names used in the translated names come from them, so the deck can be CC0 1.0 like the rest of the library. One fact in a note rests on verification sources only: for the Wadden Sea, Wikidata gives the extension years (2011 and 2014) and the three countries without qualifiers, but not that the 2014 extension was the Danish part; that is confirmed by UNESCO's secondary_dates and the Swedish Wikipedia and is stated in the compiler's own words (a fact, not expression). UNESCO's World Heritage Centre (mixed terms: CC BY-SA 3.0 IGO for © UNESCO material, but a linking policy that forbids replicating content; recorded as unknown), Riksantikvarieämbetet (CC BY 4.0) and the Swedish Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) were used for verification only. Twelve English fronts are UNESCO's official names of the properties in a form that is not a Wikidata label or alias (Church Town of Gammelstad, Luleå; Laponian Area; Agricultural Landscape of Southern Öland; Mining Area of the Great Copper Mountain in Falun; Grimeton Radio Station, Varberg; High Coast / Kvarken Archipelago; Rock Art of Alta; Vegaøyan – The Vega Archipelago; Jelling Mounds, Runic Stones and Church; Fortress of Suomenlinna; Verla Groundwood and Board Mill; Bronze Age Burial Site of Sammallahdenmäki); they are given as the names of things (facts, not expression), and no description, table or wording beyond those names was taken from UNESCO. Likewise one Swedish front, \"Parforcejaktlandskap på norra Själland\", is the title of the Swedish Wikipedia's article on the site, a name and not the article's expression; nothing else was taken from the Swedish Wikipedia; where Riksantikvarieämbetet's form of a Swedish name differs from Wikidata's, the card keeps Wikidata's. The Swedish names not in Wikidata are the compiler's own translations of UNESCO's names. The selection is the complete set of Nordic World Heritage properties, not a choice taken from any list. Quotations in the provenance report's evidence are not part of the CC0 deck: they remain under their sources' terms (Wikipedia text CC BY-SA 4.0 by Wikipedia contributors, each linked to its revision; Riksantikvarieämbetet's text CC BY 4.0; UNESCO's text under its own terms) and are short quotations given only to make the verification checkable."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every card's site, country and year against Wikidata and UNESCO's World Heritage List (2025 XML export, 2026 States Parties pages and the 48th session's new inscriptions); the Swedish names against Riksantikvarieämbetet and the Swedish Wikipedia."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards, the sources and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Wikidata and UNESCO agree on the membership of the List, the property numbers, the Nordic countries and the year of inscription of all 44 properties; no card follows a value that a better source contradicts, and no Wikidata check was skipped because it failed. The discrepancies are in Wikidata's modelling (components and duplicate items with World Heritage designations, two start times for Røros, a missing Swedish label on some newer items), in two Swedish-name forms where Riksantikvarieämbetet and Wikidata differ, and in two years in the Swedish Wikipedia's tables; they were resolved as below. The year of inscription cannot be checked by the builder (it is a qualifier) and was compared by hand for every card."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards: membership of the List, countries, years of inscription and extensions, transnational notes and Swedish names, against the review's own Wikidata query, the builder's live checks, the 2025 and 2026 sessions and national sources."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual errors; all 44 cards correct as of 2026-10-04 and the round-0 fixes confirmed. Two suggestions, both applied: the Aalto Works note now names Alvar, Aino and Elissa Aalto, and the West Norwegian Fjords front no longer contains the answer."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards, the title, the description and the keywords: language tags in the built deck, Swedish names and conventions, English names and notes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Tags, Swedish names and conventions found in order and the round-0 language fixes confirmed. One warning (an undocumented spelling difference for Røros) is now documented; of three suggestions, the en dash and the Tingvalla note were applied and the Møns Klint / Stevns klint capitalisation was kept with a reason."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards, the sources and their licence pages, the evidence quotations and locators, the method, queries, selection and licensing paragraph."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Licences, evidence and checks confirmed; the review found the documentation overstating how much came from Wikidata alone. All findings were verified and fixed: the description, licensing and source entries now name the twelve UNESCO names and the one Swedish Wikipedia name on the fronts; extension years are sourced from Wikidata P793, and the Moravian 2024 extension from a public-domain U.S. National Park Service release (new content source); the Aalto and Skogskyrkogården notes now rest on Wikidata; UNESCO locators link the Internet Archive captures read; the helper scripts' logic is in the queries; UNESCO quotations are trimmed."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards (every card had changed in rounds 1–3), the builder's 350 live Wikidata checks, validate_sources.ts, the TTL and report against the dossier, Nordic inscriptions since 2024 by live Wikidata query, and the fixes claimed in rounds 1–3."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors found: membership (including Møns Klint 2025, Aalto Works and the Bony Fish Fossils of the Western Limfjord 2026), years, extensions, Swedish names, language tags and the CC0 decision were confirmed, and every fix from rounds 1–3 is present. One documentation suggestion, verified and applied: the licensing text implied that Wikidata supplies the fact that the Danish part of the Wadden Sea was added in 2014."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9259>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata: items with the heritage designation (P1435) World Heritage Site (Q9259) in the Nordic countries, their World Heritage Site IDs (P757), countries (P17), start times and English, Swedish and multilingual labels" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/>
    dcterms:title "UNESCO World Heritage Centre: the World Heritage List (XML export), the States Parties pages of Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland and Iceland, and New Inscribed Properties (48COM, 2026)" ;
    dcterms:creator "UNESCO World Heritage Centre" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://whc.unesco.org/en/disclaimer/ (Internet Archive capture of 2026-09-22, https://web.archive.org/web/20260922044945/https://whc.unesco.org/en/disclaimer/), \"Terms / Policies\": under \"Intellectual Property\" the licence for \"© UNESCO\" material is given as \"Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 IGO\", while the \"Linking Policy\" says \"You may only link to, not replicate content.\" and \"You may not sell, license, or otherwise assign the use of any material from UNESCO’s or the World Heritage Centre’s web site.\" The terms are mixed and CC BY-SA 3.0 IGO is not among the builder's licences, so the site is recorded as \"unknown\" and used for verification only."@en .

<https://www.raa.se/evenemang-och-upplevelser/upplev-kulturarvet/varldsarv-i-sverige/>
    dcterms:title "Riksantikvarieämbetet: Världsarv i Sverige (15 världsarv i Sverige) and Frågor och svar om Världsarv" ;
    dcterms:creator "Riksantikvarieämbetet (Swedish National Heritage Board)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.raa.se/om-riksantikvarieambetet/om-webbplatsen/upphovsratt-pa-raa-se/ — \"Alla webbsidor på vår domän raa.se har generellt den friaste Creative Commons-licensen ”CC BY” där inget annat anges.\"; https://www.raa.se/om-riksantikvarieambetet/om-webbplatsen/licenser/ — \"För material med upphovsrätt använder vi i första hand Creative Commons licens CC BY, Erkännande 4.0 Sverige\"."@en .

<https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/V%C3%A4rldsarv_i_Danmark>
    dcterms:title "Svenskspråkiga Wikipedia: Världsarv i Danmark, Världsarv i Norge, Världsarv i Finland, Världsarv i Sverige, and the introductions of articles on individual sites" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikipedia contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Footer of every Swedish Wikipedia article (e.g. https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/V%C3%A4rldsarv_i_Danmark): \"Wikipedias text är tillgänglig under licensen Creative Commons Erkännande-dela-lika 4.0 Unported\""@en .

<https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1207/interior-department-celebrates-selection-of-pennsylvania-s-historic-moravian-bethlehem-district-to-unesco-world-heritage-list.htm>
    dcterms:title "U.S. National Park Service: \"Interior Department Celebrates Selection of Pennsylvania’s Historic Moravian Bethlehem District to UNESCO World Heritage List\" (news release, 26 July 2024)" ;
